From 41fb18411c85644e7c3eb0b9da7cd28e87a7ac69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 02:49:47 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 13/18] WebRender: give a promoted video's stereo mark to its
 compositor surface

A video WebRender cannot hand to the compositor as it is (any video with
software WebRender, or a picture in plain YCbCr buffers, such as VP9
decoded by libvpx) is drawn by WebRender into a native compositor surface
of its own. Such a surface now carries the picture's stereo mark, so
that it is decided on and declared like a DMABuf video.

The mark travels with the image: ImageDescriptorFlags::STEREO (bits 8 to
23 of the descriptor's flags; WrImageDescriptor.stereo from Gecko) holds
the picture's StereoMode, where it comes from and whether it is video
(EncodeStereoMark in ImageTypes.h). A YCbCr buffer texture sets it from
its descriptor. When the tile cache draws such an image into a native
surface it tells the compositor once per change
(NativeSurfaceOperationDetails::SetSurfaceStereo,
Compositor::set_surface_stereo, wr_compositor_set_surface_stereo), and
RenderCompositorNative gives it to the surface's layers
(NativeLayer::SetStereoMark).

NativeLayerWayland decides on every layer the way it did on external
layers (DecideStereoLocked moves to the base class). A layer WebRender
draws into is declared when the picture holds its views side by side
with the left eye first, visible across their whole width.

Video with opacity or a rotation is not promoted by WebRender and stays
flat: it is drawn into the page's own tiles.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
